GitHub Issue:
Post Date: 2018-08-01T16:45:34Z
Current Date: April 4 2025
Title: Why does the generic EnumerationGraphType<TEnum> use the name of TEnum as the graphql type name? - inconsistent with ObjectGraphType
Description: This is regarding a line in `graphql-dotnet/src/GraphQL/Types/EnumerationGraphType.cs`  that sets the name to the name of the underlying TEnum
```
public EnumerationGraphType()
{
    var type = typeof(TEnum);

    Name = Name ?? StringUtils.ToPascalCase(type.Name);
...
```

I'm not sure why that name is being set there. Here is an example of it causing a problem:
```
public class Organisation
{
    public enum AccessLevel { Admin, Member }
}

public class Project
{
    public enum AccessLevel { Admin, Member, ReadOnly }
}

public class OrganisationAccessLevelEnum : EnumerationGraphType<Organisation.AccessLevel>
{
    public AccessLevelEnum()
    {
        AddValue("ADMIN", "", Organisation.AccessLevel.Admin);
        AddValue("MEMBER", "", Organisation.AccessLevel.Member);
    }
}

public class ProjectAccessLevelEnum : EnumerationGraphType<Project.AccessLevel>
{
    public AccessLevelEnum()
    {
        AddValue("ADMIN", "", Organisation.AccessLevel.Admin);
        AddValue("MEMBER", "", Organisation.AccessLevel.Member);
        AddValue("READ_ONLY", "", Organisation.AccessLevel.ReadOnly);
    }
}
```
Both of those enums will get named "AccessLevel".

I'm able to avoid it by removing the generic like this:
```
public class ProjectAccessLevelEnum : EnumerationGraphType
{
    public AccessLevelEnum()
    {
        AddValue("ADMIN", "", Organisation.AccessLevel.Admin);
        AddValue("MEMBER", "", Organisation.AccessLevel.Member);
        AddValue("READ_ONLY", "", Organisation.AccessLevel.ReadOnly);
    }
}
```
which will use the name "ProjectAccessLevelEnum"

My question is: Why does the generic version of EnumerationGraphType work this way? -  the generic version of ObjectGraphType does not do this. 

